      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine the month in which Hal will change the batteries for the 25th time, we start by noting that the batteries are replaced every 5 months starting from May.\n\nFirst, let's calculate the month in which the batteries will be changed on the \\( n \\)-th replacement. We observe that the batteries are changed in the 5th, 10th, 15th, 20th, and so on, months.\n\nWe need to find the month 25 times after May. We can express the \\( n \\)-th replacement month in general form by using the formula for the \\( n \\)-th multiple of 5 plus the initial month (May).\n\nThe formula for the \\( n \\)-th replacement month \\( M_n \\) is:\n\\[ M_n = 5n + 5 \\text{ (since May is the 5th month after February)} \\]\n\nFor \\( n = 25 \\):\n\\[ M_{25} = 5 \\times 25 + 5 = 125 + 5 = 130 \\]\n\nSince we count from May, we need to find the month 130 days after May. We know that a month has approximately 31 days.\n\nWe calculate the number of full months in 130 days:\n\\[ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{130}{31} \\right\\rfloor = 4 \\text{ full months} \\]\nThis means 4 full months pass, and we have:\n\\[ 130 - 4 \\times 31 = 130 - 124 = 6 \\text{ days left} \\]\n\nSince January has 31 days, a full month of 31 days is:\n\\[ 31 - 6 = 25 \\text{ days left} \\]\n\nTherefore, 25 days after January is:\n\\[ 25 + 25 = 50 \\text{ days after March} \\Rightarrow \\text{May} \\]\n\nSo, Hal will change the batteries for the 25th time in \\(\\boxed{\\text{May}}\\).",
